  det JF == {P,Q}: True
  {P_(w=2), Q_(w=-2)} has pure weight 0: True (bracket weight-additive)

=== P2: hyperbolic equivariant Keller => LINEAR (boxeph-S144) ===
  (a,b)=(1,1): nonconstant f,g deg2 -> det constant? False (expect False: leading coeff survives)
  (a,b)=(2,1): nonconstant f,g deg2 -> det constant? False (expect False: leading coeff survives)
  (a,b)=(1,2): nonconstant f,g deg2 -> det constant? False (expect False: leading coeff survives)
  (a,b)=(3,1): nonconstant f,g deg2 -> det constant? False (expect False: leading coeff survives)
  (a,b)=(2,3): nonconstant f,g deg2 -> det constant? False (expect False: leading coeff survives)
  a=b=1 telescoping: det = d/ds[s*f*g] verified structurally (fg=const forced => LINEAR).

=== P3: elliptic equivariant Keller => INVERTIBLE (triangular) ===
  (1,2) F=(x, y+3x^2): det={(0, 0): 1} Keller=True inverse polynomial(deg<=8)=True
  (1,3) F=(x, y+2x^3): det={(0, 0): 1} Keller=True inverse polynomial(deg<=8)=True
  (2,3) F=(x+.., y..)  triangular: det={(0, 0): 1} Keller=True inverse polynomial(deg<=8)=True
  (1,2) F=(x, y+x^2): det={(0, 0): 1} Keller=True inverse polynomial(deg<=8)=True
  => all elliptic samples invertible (triangular). General argument in THM-1345 s3.

=== P4: 'no room at n=2' -- the unit product telescopes ===
  3D: coupled c1/c0 system, unit v(t)=1+t survives (THM-1305).
  2D: single telescoped constraint (s*f*g)'=const => fg=const => units trivial.
  The counterexample's unit CROSSING needs >=2 coupled units; dim 2 gives 1.
